-\paragraph{Verified acceptance.}
-The locally closed solver is probabilistic and does not itself provide an efficient completeness certificate.  That is not required here.  On a successful invocation its representation contains the planted nonsingular root.  On any invocation, a returned candidate is accepted only after all original equations, the recovered graph identities, the inverse pull-back, the unchanged public map, and an invertible oil system have been checked exactly.  Solver failure can therefore cause only retry or omission, never a false accepted key.
-
-\paragraph{Security interpretation.}
-The direct representation remains infeasible: one degree-$2^V$ vector already requires roughly $0.102$ EiB, $2.23\times10^6$ EiB, and $1.84\times10^{14}$ EiB at the three levels.  The contribution is not a practical break.  It is a structural attack whose modern and epsilon-free solver lines both lie below all category targets, now backed by an explicit-prefactor solver specialization and a separate local recovery theorem.  The epsilon-free line remains below Level I even after two generic logarithmic sensitivities.  In our assessment, all three levels should be reoptimized; retaining any level unchanged now requires a technical rebuttal to the relevant algebraic and solver interfaces, not merely an appeal to infeasible memory.
+The normalized rows are below all three category targets.  The conclusion is strongest at Levels III and V, where several independent solver specializations leave substantial margin.  Level I is more sensitive to hidden constants and representation choices.  Rather than repeating these qualifications throughout the paper, \cref{sec:security} collects them in one place.
 
-\paragraph{Roadmap.}
-\Cref{sec:model} fixes the expanded-key model, universal-forgery consequence, and exact signing certificate.  \Cref{sec:recovery} develops graph-direction completion and derivative-kernel recovery.  \Cref{sec:local-geometry} proves that Jacobian localization supplies the locally closed solver hypotheses.  \Cref{sec:projective} proves the fast projective regularization and density theorem.  \Cref{sec:solver-attack} gives the three projective cost interfaces, the graph-local interface, and verified-restart theorems.  \Cref{sec:security} compares their parameter implications.  The appendices treat seeded expansion, optional uniqueness, and executable checks.
+\subsection*{Main contributions}
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item We prove the graph-direction identity \eqref{eq:intro-translation} and show that one regular planted root determines the entire hidden graph through public linear algebra.
+\item We give an explicit Wronskian rank condenser, prove its optimal list length, and obtain deterministic category-scale lists of $315,612,1228$ square systems.
+\item We give a basis-first signing search using fewer than $1.008$ tests on average, short category-scale deterministic signing lists, and a stronger projective-line fallback.
+\item We prove that localizing at one nonzero derivative determinant supplies the regularity and radicality required by a locally closed Kronecker solver.  No assumption is made about singular components outside that open set.
+\item We prove a separate projective regularization theorem and explicit public-random bounds for all associated bad-key loci.
+\item We give finite-invocation solver failure bounds, verified restart, exact resolution checks, exact graph and pull-back checks, and a one-invocation universal-forgery statement.
+\item We distinguish theorem-backed asymptotic costs from diagnostic normalizations, and we quantify the otherwise hidden memory cost.
+\end{enumerate}
 
-\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}
+\paragraph{Terminology.}
+A \emph{regular root} is a root whose derivative matrix has full rank.  A \emph{projective slice} is a linear subspace used to reduce the hidden oil space to one point.  A \emph{geometric} or \emph{Kronecker resolution} is a univariate encoding of all roots.  A theorem described as \emph{key by key} applies to every fixed expanded key satisfying its explicit algebraic conditions; it does not assume a key distribution.  The notation $\widetilde O(\cdot)$ suppresses logarithmic factors and constants.
 
-\paragraph{QR-UOV and its attack estimates.}
-\QRUOV{} compresses UOV through a quotient-ring representation~\cite{QRUOV2021}.  The Round-2 specification analyzes direct MQ, reconciliation, intersection, PXL, and rectangular-MinRank attacks~\cite{QRUOVSpec}.  A July 2026 journal treatment by the \QRUOV{} team consolidates the scheme's security arguments, parameter choices, and implementations~\cite{FurueEtAl2026}.  Rectangular-MinRank analyses by Furue--Ikematsu and Suzuki et al.~\cite{FurueIkematsu,Suzuki} report key-recovery costs; Suzuki et al.\ use the specification's Boolean-gate conversion and give $160,219,277$ for the Round-2 $q=127$ sets.  These do not improve the specification's $158,219,277$ key-recovery rows.  Truncated-polynomial-ring work improves several UOV parameter sets but reports no improvement for the \QRUOV{} sets analyzed here~\cite{FurueIkematsu2026}.
+\paragraph{Organization.}
+\Cref{sec:model} fixes the expanded-key model and exact signing certificate.  \Cref{sec:recovery} gives graph-direction completion and the short deterministic lists.  \Cref{sec:local-geometry} proves the localization lemma.  \Cref{sec:projective} develops the faster projective route.  \Cref{sec:solver-attack} states the solver interfaces and recovery theorems.  \Cref{sec:security} gives the cost and parameter analysis.  The appendices treat seeded expansion, optional uniqueness, alternative elimination ideas, and reproducibility.
 
-Post-specification work also revisits generic underdetermined-MQ \emph{forgery}.  \emph{Just Guess} gives new classical and quantum estimates for \QRUOV{} and related schemes~\cite{JustGuess}; for the main $q=127$ sets, the \QRUOV{} team reports classical exponents $256,398,560$, including polynomial overhead~\cite{QRUOVJustGuess}.  These are forgery estimates, not direct comparators for equivalent-key recovery.  A generalized variable-partitioning preprint likewise studies underdetermined MQ~\cite{Asanuma2026}.  NIST IR~8610 concluded after Round 2 that subsequent cryptanalysis had not diminished \QRUOV{}'s security profile~\cite{NISTIR8610}.  Because estimates continue to evolve, our tables identify the Round-2 specification rows rather than claiming a timeless best known attack.
+\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}
 
-\paragraph{Geometric recovery and polynomial solving.}
-Beullens' intersection attack and subsequent UOV cryptanalysis recover hidden oil geometry by structured linear algebra~\cite{Beullens}.  P\'ebereau proved that one suitable oil vector suffices for polynomial-time expanded-key recovery~\cite{Pebereau}.  Our projective slice produces such a vector, while the public derivative kernel recovers the full oil space.
+\paragraph{QR-UOV and generic forgery attacks.}
